  "response": "Sociologists adjudicate between these accounts by asking whether the association between neighborhood poverty and immobility survives serious attempts to hold family background fixed. The Coleman Report concluded that neighborhood composition explained most of the variation in adult earnings. Families residing in high-poverty neighborhoods differ from other families in unmeasured traits that bear on their children's later earnings. At least one of two processes must therefore be generating the correlation, and perhaps both are: that familial selectivity, or a genuinely contextual mechanism whereby residence in a high-poverty neighborhood during childhood itself lowers a person's adult earnings. Coleman's compositional conclusion shows that the second, contextual mechanism is operating.\n\nPatrick Sharkey documented that more than 70 percent of Black families living in the poorest American neighborhoods remained in such neighborhoods a generation later. The Gautreaux program relocated Chicago public housing residents to predominantly white suburban communities. The interim Moving to Opportunity evaluation found no significant effects of relocation on adult employment or earnings. Adults in the experimental group reported better mental health and subjective well-being than controls.\n\nBy contrast with that flat labor-market verdict for adults, the long-run follow-up of the same families into tax records established that children who moved to low-poverty neighborhoods before age 13 earned about 31 percent more as young adults than their control-group counterparts. In other words, the low-poverty voucher offer raised the adult earnings of children who relocated before their teens by roughly a third. Chetty, Hendren, and Katz found that children who moved after age 13 experienced the largest earnings gains, and this late-mover advantage cannot be squared with the pre-adolescent premium just described, since the two patterns cannot both hold.\n\nRaj Chetty and Nathaniel Hendren analyzed federal tax records covering more than five million families who moved between commuting zones, and the behavior of those millions of movers shows that each additional year of childhood exposure to a better area produced a proportional gain in adult income, as Chetty and Hendren estimated. The reported superiority of post-13 moves cannot be reconciled with such a steady, year-by-year exposure gradient either; one of the two must be abandoned. Whereas that gradient was recovered from the interstate migration of millions of tax filers across commuting zones, identification in other designs is secured within the household or by lottery. Sibling comparison designs hold constant characteristics shared by children raised in the same family. Random assignment of housing vouchers eliminates correlation between treatment status and unmeasured family characteristics. Adjudication thus rests less on any single study than on whether a research design can hold family background fixed while allowing place to vary.",
